WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.120
Defensive refactoring is often the practical path to better bounds checking.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:15.060
Many C codebases contain useful legacy code with repeated indexes, magic numbers, hand-written copies, local assumptions, and APIs that predate current review standards.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.760
The goal is to improve safety without pretending the whole codebase can be rewritten at once.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:26.540
Replace magic numbers with named constants, derived sizes, or values tied to the actual object being accessed.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.400
A literal number may be correct today, but it is hard to audit when the array changes.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:40.340
A named limit or derived count communicates intent and reduces the chance that one caller updates a buffer without updating the matching check.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.520
Centralize repeated bounds checks where patterns repeat.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:49.740
If several functions validate an offset and length in the same way, a reviewed helper can reduce divergence.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:58.280
If several functions append to the same dynamic buffer type, a single append helper can maintain count, capacity, and terminator behavior consistently.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:04.420
Small helper functions can make checked indexing, appending, copying, and reserving easier to review.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.440
The helper should not hide complexity behind a vague name.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:12.700
It should document units, accepted ranges, output behavior, and failure handling.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:17.400
A good helper makes the calling code shorter and the safety contract more obvious.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:21.780
Structured buffer metadata can keep pointer, count, and capacity together.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.000
That does not make code automatically safe, but it makes invalid states harder to represent and easier to detect.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:34.940
A struct can also carry unit conventions, ownership rules, and helper functions that operate on the buffer consistently.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:38.200
Refactor incrementally when working with legacy code.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:45.220
Start at boundaries where input enters, where buffers are copied, where strings are formed, or where repeated review findings appear.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.360
Preserve behavior with tests, then make the bounds contract clearer.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:55.660
Small, visible improvements compound into a codebase where safe access is easier to maintain.
